CHECK:AVOID_EXTERNS: extern prototypes should be avoided in .h files
#1358: FILE: include/linux/mutex.h:88:
+extern void mutex_destroy(struct mutex *lock);

Function prototypes don't need to be declared extern in .h
files.  It's assumed by the compiler and is unnecessary.

CHECK:COMPARISON_TO_NULL: Comparison to NULL could be written "__mutex_owner"
#1408: FILE: include/linux/mutex.h:144:
+	return __mutex_owner(lock) != NULL;

Comparisons to NULL in the form (foo == NULL) or (foo != NULL)
are better written as (!foo) and (foo).

WARNING:TRAILING_SEMICOLON: trailing semicolon indicates no statements, indent implies otherwise
#1661: FILE: kernel/locking/mutex.c:449:
+	if (owner & 0x03);
+		__mutex_unlock_slowpath(lock);

Macro definition should not end with a semicolon. The macro
invocation style should be consistent with function calls.
This can prevent any unexpected code paths::

  #define MAC do_something;

If this macro is used within a if else statement, like::

  if (some_condition)
          MAC;

  else
          do_something;

Then there would be a compilation error, because when the macro is
expanded there are two trailing semicolons, so the else branch gets
orphaned.

See: https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/1399671106.2912.21.camel@joe-AO725/

ERROR:TRAILING_STATEMENTS: trailing statements should be on next line
#1661: FILE: kernel/locking/mutex.c:449:
+	if (owner & 0x03);

Trailing statements (for example after any conditional) should be
on the next line.
Statements, such as::

  if (x == y) break;

should be::

  if (x == y)
          break;

